        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "24688575-ca3d-50f7-bc7e-8ef2322ce04c",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Kolozsvár, Cluj, Hungary, Romania, Verőce\n- Events: World War I, World War II, Treaty of Trianon, Second Vienna Award\n- Organizations: Sixty-Four Counties Youth Movement\n- People: László Toroczkai\n- Projects: Kolozsvár Ghetto, Magyar Sziget\n- Concepts: Hungarian irredentism, border revision\n\nFacts:\n- The Treaty of Trianon was the 1920 peace agreement that formally ended World War I between most Allied powers and the Kingdom of Hungary.\n- The Treaty of Trianon left Hungary landlocked at 93,073 square kilometers, about 28% of the 325,411 square kilometers of the pre-war Kingdom of Hungary.\n- Between the 1920 Treaty of Trianon and the 1940 Second Vienna Award, Cluj was part of Romania.\n- The Kolozsvár Ghetto was one of the lesser-known Jewish ghettos of the World War II era.\n- The Sixty-Four Counties Youth Movement is a far-right movement in Hungary that advocates unifying ethnic Hungarians outside Hungary and revising the 1920 Treaty of Trianon.\n- Magyar Sziget is a nationalist festival in Verőce organized by the Sixty-Four Counties Youth Movement under the leadership of László Toroczkai.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "c4a0ee78-b16c-5e00-9a6d-fbe4357cdfdc",
